</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2. </span>Orthogonality-Regularized Multi-layer Mixing</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Naive fusion across many layers can be inefficient because intermediate representations are often highly correlated across depth. To mitigate this effect, we project each selected layer through a lightweight per-layer transformation before aggregation. We instantiate this transformation as an Orthogonal Layer (OL), which preserves feature scale through a near-isometric mapping while encouraging less redundant layer mixing.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_paragraph">Orthogonal Layer (OL)</h5>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Fig. <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4. Methodology ‣ CoME-VL: Scaling Complementary Multi-Encoder Vision-Language Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, we apply an Orthogonal Layer (OL) to each selected layer output before aggregation. OL is a lightweight linear projection <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{i}=\mathbf{Q}_{i}\mathbf{h}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐐</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{i}=\mathbf{Q}_{i}\mathbf{h}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> whose weight <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐐</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> is constrained to be orthogonal using such that:</p>
<table id="S4.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(3)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{cases}\mathbf{Q}_{i}^{\top}\mathbf{Q}_{i}=\mathbf{I}_{d},&amp;\text{if }m\geq d,\\
\mathbf{Q}_{i}\mathbf{Q}_{i}^{\top}=\mathbf{I}_{m},&amp;\text{if }m&lt;d.\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐐</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐐</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐐</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐐</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{cases}\mathbf{Q}_{i}^{\top}\mathbf{Q}_{i}=\mathbf{I}_{d},&amp;\text{if }m\geq d,\\
\mathbf{Q}_{i}\mathbf{Q}_{i}^{\top}=\mathbf{I}_{m},&amp;\text{if }m&lt;d.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">For square matrices (<math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=d</annotation></semantics></math>), we parameterize <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐐</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> through a learned skew-symmetric matrix <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> (where <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}_{i}=-\mathbf{A}_{i}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}_{i}=-\mathbf{A}_{i}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>) via <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}_{i}=\exp(\mathbf{A}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐐</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}_{i}=\exp(\mathbf{A}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> or the Cayley transform <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}_{i}=(\mathbf{I}_{d}+\mathbf{A}_{i}/2)(\mathbf{I}_{d}-\mathbf{A}_{i}/2)^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐐</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}_{i}=(\mathbf{I}_{d}+\mathbf{A}_{i}/2)(\mathbf{I}_{d}-\mathbf{A}_{i}/2)^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. This promotes a near-isometric transformation, reduces redundant directions across layers, and improves optimization stability when learning the layer weights. We provide more details in Appendix <a href="#A1.SS1" title="A.1. Orthogonal Layer (OL) ‣ Appendix A Methods Details ‣ CoME-VL: Scaling Complementary Multi-Encoder Vision-Language Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_paragraph">Intra-encoder multi-layer aggregation.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For each encoder <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e\in\{\text{Sig},\text{DINO}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mtext>Sig</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext>DINO</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e\in\{\text{Sig},\text{DINO}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, we first normalize each layer output, project it with OL, and then take a weighted sum across the selected set:</p>
<table id="S4.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(4)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbf{Z}}^{(l)}_{e}=\mathrm{OL}_{e}\!\left(\mathrm{LN}(\mathbf{Z}^{(l)}_{e})\right),\qquad\mathbf{V}_{e}=\sum_{l\in\mathcal{L}_{e}}w_{l}^{(e)}\tilde{\mathbf{Z}}^{(l)}_{e}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐙</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>OL</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>LN</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐙</mi><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>l</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐙</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbf{Z}}^{(l)}_{e}=\mathrm{OL}_{e}\!\left(\mathrm{LN}(\mathbf{Z}^{(l)}_{e})\right),\qquad\mathbf{V}_{e}=\sum_{l\in\mathcal{L}_{e}}w_{l}^{(e)}\tilde{\mathbf{Z}}^{(l)}_{e}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}_{\text{Sig}}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{s}\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mtext>Sig</mtext></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}_{\text{Sig}}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{s}\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}_{\text{DINO}}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{d}\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mtext>DINO</mtext></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}_{\text{DINO}}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{d}\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> are pooled token sets in a shared hidden dimension <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> (implemented by choosing OL output dimensions to match).</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3. </span>RoPE-Enhanced Alignment</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Fusing heterogeneous encoders is challenging because their token grids can differ in resolution. SigLIP2 typically produces <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> tokens (e.g., <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="24\times 24" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>24</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>24</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">24\times 24</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="384\times 384" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>384</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>384</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">384\times 384</annotation></semantics></math>), while DINOv3 produces <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> tokens (e.g., <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="14\times 14" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>14</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>14</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">14\times 14</annotation></semantics></math> depending on preprocessing). Direct token concatenation is a simple fusion strategy, but it increases the number of visual tokens processed by the LLM and therefore raises compute and memory cost. In contrast, our RoPE-enhanced cross-attention aligns heterogeneous encoder features more efficiently while preserving spatial correspondence; as shown in Table <a href="#S5.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 5.3. Qualitative Analysis ‣ 5. Experiments ‣ CoME-VL: Scaling Complementary Multi-Encoder Vision-Language Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, CoME-VL increases inference time only modestly from 1.26s to 1.52s per sample, and remains substantially more efficient than COMM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, which requires about 2.2s/sample due to direct feature concatenation.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_paragraph">Geometry-aware cross-attention with RoPE.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use SigLIP2 tokens as queries and DINOv3 tokens as keys and values. To encourage spatially consistent matching, we incorporate RoPE into the attention computation so that attention scores depend on relative spatial offsets:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(5)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐐</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{Q}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathrm{RoPE}(\mathbf{W}_{Q}\,\mathrm{LN}(\mathbf{V}_{\text{Sig}}))," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>RoPE</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>LN</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mtext>Sig</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathrm{RoPE}(\mathbf{W}_{Q}\,\mathrm{LN}(\mathbf{V}_{\text{Sig}})),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(6)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐊</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{K}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathrm{RoPE}(\mathbf{W}_{K}\,\mathrm{LN}(\mathbf{V}_{\text{DINO}}))," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>RoPE</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>LN</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mtext>DINO</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathrm{RoPE}(\mathbf{W}_{K}\,\mathrm{LN}(\mathbf{V}_{\text{DINO}})),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(7)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐕</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{V}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbf{W}_{V}\,\mathrm{LN}(\mathbf{V}_{\text{DINO}})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>LN</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mtext>DINO</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbf{W}_{V}\,\mathrm{LN}(\mathbf{V}_{\text{DINO}}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">followed by standard scaled dot-product attention. The cross-attention output is combined with SigLIP2 features via a gated residual connection. We follow Flamingo <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Alayrac et al., 2022</a>)</cite> for gated attention in visual fusion:</p>
<table id="S4.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(8)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}_{\text{fused}}=\mathbf{V}_{\text{Sig}}+\tanh(\gamma)\cdot\mathrm{CrossAttn}(\mathbf{V}_{\text{Sig}},\mathbf{V}_{\text{DINO}})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mtext>fused</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mtext>Sig</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>tanh</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>CrossAttn</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mtext>Sig</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mtext>DINO</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}_{\text{fused}}=\mathbf{V}_{\text{Sig}}+\tanh(\gamma)\cdot\mathrm{CrossAttn}(\mathbf{V}_{\text{Sig}},\mathbf{V}_{\text{DINO}}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> is initialized to zero to stabilize early training and gradually enables DINOv3 information to be incorporated.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, the projected visual tokens produced by the RGCA cross-attention block (Fig. <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4. Methodology ‣ CoME-VL: Scaling Complementary Multi-Encoder Vision-Language Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) are forwarded to the decoder-only LLM. Compared with concatenation followed by self-attention, this cross-attention interface injects a compact set of visual tokens and avoids increasing the LLM context length, making the fusion more compute- and memory-efficient while preserving the aligned visual evidence needed for generation and grounding. For bounding-box generation, our baseline Molmo <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Deitke et al., 2025</a>)</cite> does not natively support object detection. We therefore redesign the fine-tuning output format to enable box prediction (see Fig. <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4. Methodology ‣ CoME-VL: Scaling Complementary Multi-Encoder Vision-Language Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> for the target format). For more details see Appendix <a href="#A1.SS3" title="A.3. Bounding-box encoding as tokens ‣ Appendix A Methods Details ‣ CoME-VL: Scaling Complementary Multi-Encoder Vision-Language Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2604.03231v1/qualitative_analysis.png" id="S4.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/85;" width="476" height="85" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4. </span>Qualitative results on PixMo pointing. Compared to prior VLMs, CoME-VL demonstrates more precise coordinate-level grounding for fine-grained visual queries.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S4.F5" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2604.03231v1/tasks.png" id="S4.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/71;" width="476" height="71" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5. </span>Qualitative examples of CoME-VL on chart understanding, document/table reasoning, localization, pointing, and counting, demonstrating its ability to jointly support visual understanding and grounding.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5. </span>Experiments</h2>

<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1. </span>Experimetal Setup</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We build on Molmo <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Deitke et al., 2025</a>)</cite> with Qwen2-7B (3584-dim, 28 layers) as the language backbone. Vision encoding uses SigLIP-2-SO400M (<math id="S5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="384{\times}384" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>384</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>384</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">384{\times}384</annotation></semantics></math>, patch 16, 27 layers) for semantic features and frozen DINOv3-Large (<math id="S5.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="224{\times}224" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>224</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>224</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">224{\times}224</annotation></semantics></math>, 24 layers) for spatial features. Features are fused via 4D Cross-RoPE attention with <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2{\times}2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2{\times}2</annotation></semantics></math> pooling and MLP projection. Images are processed at native resolution with overlap-and-resize cropping (max 12 crops). Across all settings, we freeze DINO parameters and update SigLIP parameters during training.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Training uses AdamW (<math id="S5.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta{=}(0.9,0.95)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0.9</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.95</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta{=}(0.9,0.95)</annotation></semantics></math>, weight decay <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.01</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.01</annotation></semantics></math>) with a base learning rate of <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{-5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{-5}</annotation></semantics></math> for the LLM, SigLIP, and connector modules. A cosine learning-rate schedule with 100 warmup steps is applied. Training is conducted with a global batch size of 24, sequence length 2304, BF16 precision, and Fully Sharded Data Parallel (FSDP). The model is trained using <span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">64<math id="S5.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>AMD Instinct MI210</span> GPUs (64 GB each) for 7 days. The training data consists of the Pixmo dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Deitke et al., 2025</a>)</cite> and RefCOCO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mao et al., 2016</a>)</cite>. Additional implementation details are provided in Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Methods Details ‣ CoME-VL: Scaling Complementary Multi-Encoder Vision-Language Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>, including the Orthogonal Layer design, its optimization parameterization, the redundancy metric, and the bounding-box generation setup used for RefCOCO.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We use the Molmo-defined accuracy metrics, including F1-cap; for Pixmo pointing, we report 3-pixel and 5-pixel counting accuracy, where a prediction is considered correct if its distance to the ground truth is within 3 px or 5 px, respectively, and accuracy is computed as the ratio of correct predictions to the total; RefCOCO evaluation follows the original experimental setup <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kazemzadeh et al., 2014</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2. </span>Quantitative Analysis</h3>

<section id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_paragraph">Comparison with State of the Art VLMs.</h5>

<div id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In Table <a href="#S1.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ CoME-VL: Scaling Complementary Multi-Encoder Vision-Language Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, we compare our method with state-of-the-art vision–language models (VLMs) on grounding. Since our evaluation is based on the Molmo baseline, which is built upon Qwen2-VL, we restrict comparisons to VLMs released on or before Qwen2-VL to ensure a fair comparison in visual understanding and text generation on the PixMo dataset.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ Entropy-informed candidate layer selection. ‣ 4.1. Multi-layer Visual Representations ‣ 4. Methodology ‣ CoME-VL: Scaling Complementary Multi-Encoder Vision-Language Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> reports results on the PixMo benchmarks. Our model achieves 57.24% on Charts, 66.94% on Diagrams, 70.75% on Tables, and 81.84% on Others, outperforming LLaVA variants. On the Counting task, CoME-VL attains 87.83%, exceeding InternVL2-8B (74.05%) and Qwen2-VL-7B (57.42%). Notably, CoME-VL is the only one reporting Pointing metrics, achieving 58.56% and 75.94% accuracy under @3px and @5px thresholds, respectively.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">On RefCOCO (Table <a href="#S5.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ Comparison with feature-merging methods. ‣ 5.2. Quantitative Analysis ‣ 5. Experiments ‣ CoME-VL: Scaling Complementary Multi-Encoder Vision-Language Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>), our method achieves 92.57% on the validation set, 95.36% on testA, and 90.51% on testB, outperforming both Qwen2-VL and Clip-to-DINO baselines.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_paragraph">Comparison with feature-merging methods.</h5>

<div id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S5.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ Comparison with feature-merging methods. ‣ 5.2. Quantitative Analysis ‣ 5. Experiments ‣ CoME-VL: Scaling Complementary Multi-Encoder Vision-Language Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> compares our method against the state-of-the-art feature-merging approach CLIP-to-DINO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al., 2023</a>)</cite> and our VLM baselines on RefCOCO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kazemzadeh et al., 2014</a>)</cite> using the original <span id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">val</span>, <span id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">testA</span>, and <span id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">testB</span> splits.
We report the percentage of correct predictions (%), where a prediction is correct if IoU <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≥</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq</annotation></semantics></math> 0.5.
Our method achieves the best performance across all splits, improving over CLIP-to-DINO by +0.84 (val), +1.30 (testA), and +1.66 (testB).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T3" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 3. </span>Performance comparison on the RefCOCO benchmark. Results are reported as localization accuracy (%) on the val, testA, and testB splits.</figcaption>
<table id="S5.T3.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S5.T3.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T3.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:0.9pt;padding-bottom:0.9pt;"><span id="S5.T3.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">RefCOCO</span></th>
<th id="S5.T3.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:0.9pt;padding-bottom:0.9pt;"><span id="S5.T3.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">val</span></th>
<th id="S5.T3.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:0.9pt;padding-bottom:0.9pt;"><span id="S5.T3.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">testA</span></th>
<th id="S5.T3.2.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:0.9pt;padding-bottom:0.9pt;"><span id="S5.T3.2.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">testB</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S5.T3.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T3.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:0.9pt;padding-bottom:0.9pt;"><span id="S5.T3.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Molmo <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Deitke et al., 2025</a>)</cite></span></th>
<td id="S5.T3.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:0.9pt;padding-bottom:0.9pt;"><span id="S5.T3.2.2.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">0.10</span></td>
<td id="S5.T3.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:0.9pt;padding-bottom:0.9pt;"><span id="S5.T3.2.2.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">0.27</span></td>
<td id="S5.T3.2.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:0.9pt;padding-bottom:0.9pt;"><span id="S5.T3.2.2.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">0.27</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T3.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T3.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row" style="padding-top:0.9pt;padding-bottom:0.9pt;"><span id="S5.T3.2.3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Clip-to-DINO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al., 2023</a>)</cite></span></th>
<td id="S5.T3.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:0.9pt;padding-bottom:0.9pt;"><span id="S5.T3.2.3.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">91.73</span></td>
<td id="S5.T3.2.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:0.9pt;padding-bottom:0.9pt;"><span id="S5.T3.2.3.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">94.06</span></td>
<td id="S5.T3.2.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:0.9pt;padding-bottom:0.9pt;"><span id="S5.T3.2.3.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">88.85</span></td></tr>
