<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Methods Details</h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given an image <math id="A1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> and a referring text query <math id="A1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, our goal is to predict the bounding box <math id="A1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b=[x_{\min},y_{\min},x_{\max},y_{\max}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b=[x_{\min},y_{\min},x_{\max},y_{\max}]</annotation></semantics></math> that localizes the region in <math id="A1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> described by <math id="A1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>. We formulate this as an autoregressive generation task where the language model produces bounding box coordinates as specialized tokens.</p>
</div>
<section id="A1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.1. </span>Orthogonal Layer (OL)</h3>

<div id="A1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Multi-layer fusion can be redundant because intermediate representations in deep encoders are often highly correlated across depth. Our goal is to (i) preserve useful information from multiple depths while (ii) reducing overlap among the directions contributed by different layers. To this end, we introduce an <em id="A1.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Orthogonal Layer</em> (OL): a lightweight linear projection applied to each selected layer output before mixing. OL is constrained to be (semi-)orthogonal so that it acts as a near-isometry (no degenerate scaling) and encourages the mixed features to occupy complementary subspaces.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let encoder <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> produce a sequence of hidden states at layer <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math>:
<math id="A1.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Z}_{e}^{(l)}\in\mathbb{R}^{T\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐙</mi><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Z}_{e}^{(l)}\in\mathbb{R}^{T\times d}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of tokens and <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is the hidden dimension.
We select a subset of layers <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}_{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}_{e}</annotation></semantics></math> for mixing (e.g., evenly spaced layers). For each selected layer <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l\in\mathcal{S}_{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l\in\mathcal{S}_{e}</annotation></semantics></math>, we apply LayerNorm and then an orthogonal projection to obtain <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\mathbf{Z}}_{e}^{(l)}\in\mathbb{R}^{T\times m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\mathbf{Z}}_{e}^{(l)}\in\mathbb{R}^{T\times m}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> is the mixing dimension (often <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=d</annotation></semantics></math> in our implementation).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.2. </span>Layer aggregation (mixing)</h3>

<div id="A1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">After OL, we mix the selected layer features with learned nonnegative weights:</p>
<table id="A1.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(9)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Z}_{e}=\sum_{l\in\mathcal{S}_{e}}\alpha_{e,l}\,\widehat{\mathbf{Z}}_{e}^{(l)},\qquad\alpha_{e,l}=\frac{\exp(s_{e,l})}{\sum_{k\in\mathcal{S}_{e}}\exp(s_{e,k})}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐙</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Z}_{e}=\sum_{l\in\mathcal{S}_{e}}\alpha_{e,l}\,\widehat{\mathbf{Z}}_{e}^{(l)},\qquad\alpha_{e,l}=\frac{\exp(s_{e,l})}{\sum_{k\in\mathcal{S}_{e}}\exp(s_{e,k})},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{e,l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{e,l}</annotation></semantics></math> are learned scalars (one per selected layer). The softmax constraint ensures stability and interpretability (weights sum to <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>).
We then use <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Z}_{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐙</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Z}_{e}</annotation></semantics></math> as the mixed representation for downstream modules.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.3. </span>Bounding-box encoding as tokens</h3>

<div id="A1.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Molmo <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Deitke et al., 2025</a>)</cite> does not natively support detection outputs. To enable autoregressive box prediction, we serialize each bounding box into a short sequence of discrete coordinate tokens.</p>
</div>
<section id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_paragraph">Box parameterization and ordering.</h5>

<div id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Each box is represented in <em id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">corner form</em> as <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{1},y_{1},x_{2},y_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{1},y_{1},x_{2},y_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{1},y_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{1},y_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> the top-left and <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{2},y_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{2},y_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> the bottom-right corner.
We enforce <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1}\leq x_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1}\leq x_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{1}\leq y_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{1}\leq y_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> by construction during data preprocessing and decoding (clamping if needed).
The token order is fixed as:</p>
<table id="A1.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(10)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle\texttt{BOX}\rangle\;\;\langle x_{1}\rangle\;\;\langle y_{1}\rangle\;\;\langle x_{2}\rangle\;\;\langle y_{2}\rangle\;\;\langle\texttt{END\_BOX}\rangle." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">BOX</mtext><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.560em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.560em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.560em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.560em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.560em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">END_BOX</mtext><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle\texttt{BOX}\rangle\;\;\langle x_{1}\rangle\;\;\langle y_{1}\rangle\;\;\langle x_{2}\rangle\;\;\langle y_{2}\rangle\;\;\langle\texttt{END\_BOX}\rangle.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_paragraph">Normalization.</h5>

<div id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Coordinates are normalized to <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to the input image size <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(W,H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(W,H)</annotation></semantics></math> used for training/evaluation:</p>
<table id="A1.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(11)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{x}=\frac{x}{W},\qquad\hat{y}=\frac{y}{H}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>x</mi><mi>W</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>y</mi><mi>H</mi></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{x}=\frac{x}{W},\qquad\hat{y}=\frac{y}{H},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">so each <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{x},\hat{y}\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{x},\hat{y}\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_paragraph">Discretization (bins) and vocabulary.</h5>

<div id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We quantize each normalized coordinate into <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> uniform bins (we use <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=1000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=1000</annotation></semantics></math> unless otherwise stated):</p>
<table id="A1.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(12)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(\hat{u})=\left\lfloor\mathrm{clip}(\hat{u},0,1)\cdot(B-1)+0.5\right\rfloor\in\{0,\ldots,B-1\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>⌊</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>clip</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><mo>⌋</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(\hat{u})=\left\lfloor\mathrm{clip}(\hat{u},0,1)\cdot(B-1)+0.5\right\rfloor\in\{0,\ldots,B-1\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Each integer bin index is mapped to a dedicated token from a reserved coordinate vocabulary
<math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\langle\texttt{COORD\_0}\rangle,\ldots,\langle\texttt{COORD\_}(B-1)\rangle\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">COORD_0</mtext><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">COORD_</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\langle\texttt{COORD\_0}\rangle,\ldots,\langle\texttt{COORD\_}(B-1)\rangle\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle x_{1}\rangle=\langle\texttt{COORD\_}q(\hat{x}_{1})\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">COORD_</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle x_{1}\rangle=\langle\texttt{COORD\_}q(\hat{x}_{1})\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> and similarly for <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{1},x_{2},y_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{1},x_{2},y_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_paragraph">Decoding back to pixels.</h5>

<div id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given predicted bin indices <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in\{0,\ldots,B-1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in\{0,\ldots,B-1\}</annotation></semantics></math>, we de-quantize to normalized coordinates</p>
<table id="A1.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(13)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{u}=\frac{k}{B-1}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{u}=\frac{k}{B-1},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">and recover pixel coordinates as <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{x}=\tilde{u}W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{x}=\tilde{u}W</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{y}=\tilde{u}H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{y}=\tilde{u}H</annotation></semantics></math> (followed by clipping to image bounds).
If the model outputs invalid corner ordering (rare), we swap endpoints to ensure <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1}\leq x_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1}\leq x_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{1}\leq y_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{1}\leq y_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_paragraph">Autoregressive factorization.</h5>

<div id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">With the above serialization, the probability of a single box token sequence factorizes autoregressively as</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="A1.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(14)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(\mathbf{y}_{\text{box}}\mid\mathbf{x})=\\
p(\texttt{BOX}\mid\mathbf{x})\prod_{t\in\{x_{1},y_{1},x_{2},y_{2}\}}p\!\left(\langle t\rangle\mid\mathbf{x},\texttt{BOX},\langle x_{1}\rangle,\ldots\right)\cdot p(\texttt{END\_BOX}\mid\cdot)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mtext>box</mtext></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">BOX</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mpadded style="width:0.163em;" width="0.163em"><mi>p</mi></mpadded><mo>(</mo><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">BOX</mtext><mo>,</mo><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo rspace="0.055em">)</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">END_BOX</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\mathbf{y}_{\text{box}}\mid\mathbf{x})=\\
p(\texttt{BOX}\mid\mathbf{x})\prod_{t\in\{x_{1},y_{1},x_{2},y_{2}\}}p\!\left(\langle t\rangle\mid\mathbf{x},\texttt{BOX},\langle x_{1}\rangle,\ldots\right)\cdot p(\texttt{END\_BOX}\mid\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">which corresponds to Eq. (9) in the appendix.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Fusion and Alignment in Multi-Encoder VLMs</h2>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A common design in recent MLLMs (e.g., LLaVA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2023a</a>)</cite> and Qwen-VL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al., 2023</a>)</cite>) is to concatenate visual tokens with text tokens and rely on self-attention in the language backbone for fusion. While effective, this approach increases the context length processed by the LLM, leading to higher compute and memory cost, and can dilute fine-grained visual evidence when the number of visual tokens is large. In contrast, cross-attention-based interfaces (e.g., BLIP-2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2022</a>)</cite>) provide a compact and controllable mechanism by allowing language or visual queries to attend to a smaller set of visual tokens without inflating LLM context length. Below we summarize the trade-offs and provide additional analysis supporting our design choices.</p>
</div>
<section id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_paragraph">Concatenation vs. cross-attention fusion.</h5>

<div id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{T}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{t}\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐓</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{T}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{t}\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> denote text tokens and <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{v}\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{v}\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> denote visual tokens (after projection to the LLM width).
<span id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Concatenation fusion</span> forms <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}=[\mathbf{T};\mathbf{V}]\in\mathbb{R}^{(N_{t}+N_{v})\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>𝐓</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>𝐕</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}=[\mathbf{T};\mathbf{V}]\in\mathbb{R}^{(N_{t}+N_{v})\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> and applies self-attention over all tokens. The per-layer attention cost scales as <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}((N_{t}+N_{v})^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}((N_{t}+N_{v})^{2})</annotation></semantics></math> and memory similarly scales with the square of the total sequence length.
<span id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Cross-attention fusion</span> keeps the LLM sequence length fixed at <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and injects visual information via attention from text queries to a (possibly compact) visual memory:</p>
<table id="A2.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(15)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CrossAttn}(\mathbf{T},\mathbf{V})=\mathrm{softmax}\!\left(\frac{\mathbf{T}\mathbf{W}_{Q}(\mathbf{V}\mathbf{W}_{K})^{\top}}{\sqrt{d}}\right)\mathbf{V}\mathbf{W}_{V}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>CrossAttn</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐓</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐕</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mpadded style="width:3.720em;" width="3.720em"><mi>softmax</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>𝐓𝐖</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐕𝐖</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><msqrt><mi>d</mi></msqrt></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐕𝐖</mi><mi>V</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CrossAttn}(\mathbf{T},\mathbf{V})=\mathrm{softmax}\!\left(\frac{\mathbf{T}\mathbf{W}_{Q}(\mathbf{V}\mathbf{W}_{K})^{\top}}{\sqrt{d}}\right)\mathbf{V}\mathbf{W}_{V},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">with complexity <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(N_{t}N_{v})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(N_{t}N_{v})</annotation></semantics></math> per cross-attention layer. When <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> is controlled (e.g., pooled tokens or a learned resampler), cross-attention yields substantially lower cost than concatenation and avoids increasing the LLM context length. This is especially relevant for multi-encoder settings, where concatenation would otherwise add multiple visual token streams.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_paragraph">Why alignment matters in multi-encoder fusion.</h5>

<div id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">When fusing heterogeneous vision encoders (e.g., contrastive SigLIP-like and self-supervised DINO-like backbones), their token grids may differ in resolution, receptive fields, and preprocessing. Direct concatenation ignores correspondences between tokens and can force the LLM to implicitly learn alignment through self-attention, which is inefficient and brittle under resolution changes. We therefore introduce an explicit alignment stage prior to LLM fusion: a vision-to-vision cross-attention connector that maps one encoder’s tokens into the coordinate frame of the other, producing an aligned and compact visual memory.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_paragraph">Geometry-aware alignment and token efficiency.</h5>

<div id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our RGCA connector aligns SigLIP queries to DINO keys/values and incorporates 2D RoPE so attention logits depend on relative spatial offsets. This design encourages spatially consistent matches across grids while keeping the output token count fixed (equal to the SigLIP token count). Optionally, we pool DINO tokens (e.g., <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2\times 2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2\times 2)</annotation></semantics></math> pooling) before cross-attention to further reduce <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> and improve robustness to patch-size mismatch. The resulting fused visual tokens are then projected and passed to the LLM, achieving efficient fusion without increasing the LLM context length.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Semantic Feature Analysis Per Layer</h2>

<div id="A3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We present a qualitative, layer-wise analysis of semantic and spatial features learned by SigLIP2 and DINOv3 using attention rollout visualizations. Instead of aggregating attention across layers or applying gradient-based weighting, we compute attention rollout independently for each transformer layer, allowing direct inspection of how visual focus evolves with depth.</p>
</div>
<section id="A3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.1. </span>Layer-wise Attention Rollout Visualization</h3>

<div id="A3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To visualize how attention evolves across depth, we extract the self-attention matrix from each transformer layer and fuse the attention heads using mean aggregation. We then prune low-importance attention links with a fixed discard ratio while preserving the class-token connections, and recursively multiply the resulting attention matrices to propagate token-to-token relevance within each layer.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The final class-to-patch attention map is reshaped to the spatial grid, normalized, and overlaid on the input image as a heatmap. Repeating this process independently for each layer produces layer-specific visualizations without cross-layer averaging or gradient-based weighting. Figures <a href="#A3.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ C.1. Layer-wise Attention Rollout Visualization ‣ Appendix C Semantic Feature Analysis Per Layer ‣ CoME-VL: Scaling Complementary Multi-Encoder Vision-Language Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>, <a href="#A3.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ C.1. Layer-wise Attention Rollout Visualization ‣ Appendix C Semantic Feature Analysis Per Layer ‣ CoME-VL: Scaling Complementary Multi-Encoder Vision-Language Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>, <a href="#A3.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ C.1. Layer-wise Attention Rollout Visualization ‣ Appendix C Semantic Feature Analysis Per Layer ‣ CoME-VL: Scaling Complementary Multi-Encoder Vision-Language Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>, and <a href="#A3.F10" title="Figure 10 ‣ C.1. Layer-wise Attention Rollout Visualization ‣ Appendix C Semantic Feature Analysis Per Layer ‣ CoME-VL: Scaling Complementary Multi-Encoder Vision-Language Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a> present the rollout results for deeper DINOv3 and SigLIP2 layers, highlighting their distinct spatial and semantic behaviors across depth.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A3.F7" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2604.03231v1/Figures/dino_1.png" id="A3.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:381/374;" width="381" height="374" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 7. </span>Layer-wise attention rollout for deeper layers: DINOv3 illustrating the transition from spatially coherent object-level attention to semantically discriminative region focus.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A3.F8" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2604.03231v1/Figures/siglip_1.png" id="A3.F8.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:381/312;" width="381" height="312" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 8. </span>Layer-wise attention rollout for deeper layers: SigLIP2 illustrating the transition from spatially coherent object-level attention to semantically discriminative region focus.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A3.F9" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2604.03231v1/Figures/dino_2.png" id="A3.F9.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:381/374;" width="381" height="374" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 9. </span>Layer-wise attention rollout for deeper layers: DINOv3 illustrating the transition from spatially coherent object-level attention to semantically discriminative region focus.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A3.F10" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2604.03231v1/Figures/siglip_2.png" id="A3.F10.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:381/319;" width="381" height="319" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 10. </span>Layer-wise attention rollout for deeper layers: SigLIP2, illustrating the transition from spatially coherent object-level attention to semantically discriminative region focus.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_paragraph">Layer-wise Analysis.</h5>

<div id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The layer-wise attention rollout reveals a clear complementarity between SigLIP2 and DINOv3. SigLIP2 exhibits richer and more informative responses in its early layers, capturing diverse semantic cues with broad spatial coverage, while DINOv3 concentrates discriminative and spatially coherent information in its deeper layers (layers 10–23). This observation directly supports our design choice in the main model: leveraging early SigLIP2 layers for semantic diversity and later DINOv3 layers for precise spatial grounding enables a balanced representation that benefits fine-grained understanding and localization tasks.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
</article>
</div>
</div>
